754
00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:28.180
And here was a chance for him to perform both in front of a camera

755
00:35:28.180 --> 00:35:30.760
and in front of a live audience of kids

756
00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.240
who at this time were all fans of The Office.

757
00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:34.700
We were in season three at this point.

758
00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:38.540
What I remember is the way these takes would go.

759
00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:40.780
Steve would perform the scene

760
00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:43.480
and then the kids in the audience would have to pretend

761
00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:46.520
that they were very unimpressed by his lecture.

762
00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:48.120
And so they would sit there in silence.

763
00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:50.680
And then the assistant director would call cut.

764
00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:54.820
And then the whole group of kids would just burst out into laughter

765
00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.280
that they'd been repressing through the whole scene.

766
00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:02.400
So over and over again, it was Steve performs, silence, cut, huge laugh.

767
00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:08.860
Then in the final take of the final scene, Steve performs, there's silence.

768
00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:11.220
The AD says, cut, that's a wrap for Steve.

769
00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.960
The audience of kids burst into laughter.

770
00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:18.100
They stand up and they give him this ovation that went on forever.

771
00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:22.980
I was standing next to Steve at the time and I saw the tears come to his eyes.

772
00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:24.200
It was so beautiful.

773
00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:27.660
One of the highlights for sure of my time on The Office.

774
00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:29.140
Oh, my gosh.

775
00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:30.160
I wish I could have been there.

776
00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:33.720
I bet it felt so magical to those kids, you know, and to Steve, too.

777
00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:37.580
He basically had this whole audience just for himself.

778
00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:43.620
And kind of what Brent was talking about, which is that Steve started in live performance.

779
00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.160
And I did, too, with theater, Angela.

780
00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:52.680
And it was a big adjustment to be sort of acting into a void, which is the camera.

781
00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:55.000
You don't get to see your jokes land.

782
00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:59.760
You don't get to hear people in their living rooms at home, you know,

783
00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:03.300
moving through your performance with you like you do in a theater situation.

784
00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:06.540
So it's always fun to get to perform for a live audience.

785
00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:07.540
Always, always.

786
00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:11.740
That was my whole, you know, every week doing an improv show.

787
00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:17.640
And it is a real energy to have a room like that with you and in the moment with you.

788
00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.120
It just always kind of makes my skin tingle.

789
00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:20.780
All right.

790
00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:23.200
So should we go check in at Dunder Mifflin now?

791
00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:30.380
Because Dwight is making a trap for the bat using a box and some glue.

792
00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:30.780
Yeah.

793
00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:31.840
So terrible.

794
00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:32.180
Poor bat.

795
00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:32.980
Yeah.

796
00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:39.620
And in the background, Jim and Karen are continuing this prank.

797
00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:40.360
Yeah.

798
00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.200
Karen is doing it, not Pam.

799
00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:44.440
I noticed.

800
00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:47.740
And I don't, I mean, I don't want to be.

801
00:37:47.960 --> 00:37:48.940
Oh, here it comes.

802
00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:50.320
I mean.

803
00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:53.140
Was Pam better at pranking?

804
00:37:54.700 --> 00:37:55.920
You said it.

805
00:37:56.980 --> 00:37:58.980
I mean, yes.

806
00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:06.720
I feel like Jim and Karen's prank is a little presentational and a little on the nose.

807
00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:16.280
And I think that when Pam pranks Dwight with Jim, it's more subtle and creative.

808
00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:17.020
I don't know.

809
00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:17.340
Yeah.

810
00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:26.040
Well, you know, it's really hard to have the finesse of like drinking your water by the water cooler and, you know,

811
00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:31.000
covering up whatever Jim is doing by drinking a little cup of water and looking around.

812
00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:32.240
I mean, that is hard.

813
00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.340
That doesn't come natural.

814
00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:34.980
I knew you were going to bring that up.

815
00:38:35.400 --> 00:38:40.720
I knew you were going to bring that up because it's the one time that Pam is terrible at pranking.

816
00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:42.820
It makes me laugh.

817
00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:43.800
It makes me laugh.

818
00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:45.680
But I'm sorry.

819
00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:48.400
Her other pranks are very good.

820
00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:55.320
But yes, so they have this whole bit where like Jim can't pick up her piece of bread.

821
00:38:55.480 --> 00:38:56.260
It's white hot.

822
00:38:56.560 --> 00:38:57.500
Because it's garlic bread.

823
00:38:58.020 --> 00:38:59.580
And it's burning his hands.

824
00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:01.580
And she's like, this is ice cold.

825
00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:09.460
And then we go into, I mean, it's a little bit of heartbreak in the break room again.

826
00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:10.140
With Toby?

827
00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:10.940
But it's Toby.

828
00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:11.680
Oh, yeah.

829
00:39:11.980 --> 00:39:13.280
It's Toby has some heartbreak.

830
00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:16.380
I mean, you guys, Toby is in love with Pam.

831
00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:25.340
And here is his opportunity to see her outside of work and maybe dazzle her somehow with his knowledge of art and support her.

832
00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:28.960
And it's the same evening of his daughter's play.

833
00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:30.180
He's devastated.

834
00:39:30.180 --> 00:39:34.840
And he's even trying to think of a way to get out of going to see his child's play.

835
00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:36.220
I know.

836
00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:39.140
He's like, it's just we need to support local art.

837
00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:43.200
And what they're doing is not art.

838
00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:44.640
Let's be honeSt.

839
00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:49.440
And Pam's like, you should go see your child's play.

840
00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:50.940
Yes.

841
00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:52.760
Yes.

842
00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:54.000
Oh.

843
00:39:54.720 --> 00:40:02.140
Well, lady, while this is going on, back at the school, they're starting to get into the Q&A part, right?

844
00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:04.880
And the students are starting to ask Michael questions.

845
00:40:05.080 --> 00:40:06.340
Things are kind of falling apart.

846
00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:09.020
There's a moment, Jenna, I love so much.

847
00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:14.060
I love it when Michael says, real business is done on paper.

848
00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:15.300
Write that down.

849
00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:16.740
And they all type.

850
00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:18.340
Yeah.

851
00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:19.860
It's just the sound of keyboards.

852
00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:21.540
Yes.

853
00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:25.920
That's a very fun joke in the script, too.

854
00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:29.680
Like, it says the students all get out their computers and begin typing.

855
00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:32.400
It's so good.

856
00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:39.820
So the students start asking him, how would you keep a client from leaving Dunder Mifflin for a nationwide chain?

857
00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:44.660
And Michael's like, I don't need to persuade them to come back.

858
00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:50.120
They'll just come back eventually when they realize what a debacle it is to go to anyone else.

859
00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:53.160
And then the student's like, well, has anyone ever come back?

860
00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.860
And Michael's like, I don't want them back because they're stupid.

861
00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:58.680
Michael is starting to take this personal.

862
00:40:59.240 --> 00:41:00.760
He's getting his feelings hurt.

863
00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:09.700
And that is when one of the students points out, even your own employee says that Dunder Mifflin is going to be obsolete in five to ten years.

864
00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:11.060
Oh.

865
00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:14.760
I mean, this is just like a sucker punch.

866
00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:17.160
Michael is so hurt.

867
00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:17.660
Yeah.

868
00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:17.710
Yeah.

869
00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:23.660
So now, back at the office, Dwight has formed an allegiance with Creed.

870
00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:27.360
What I love about this, Jenna, is that Dwight couldn't even get a sentence out.

871
00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.580
He was like, listen, Creed, I need an alliance.

872
00:41:29.680 --> 00:41:30.120
He's like, sure.

873
00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:34.200
Like, Creed is just like, rats to go.

874
00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:36.000
He is up for it.

875
00:41:36.100 --> 00:41:37.640
He has a drawer of tools.

876
00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:38.920
So does Dwight.

877
00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:40.780
I mean, they're meant to be.

878
00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:44.860
Dwight asks him, can you turn a wooden mop handle into a stake?

879
00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:46.880
And Creed is like, what size?

880
00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:47.220
Yeah.

881
00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:50.980
He's got a drawer of mung beans and weaponry, apparently.

882
00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:58.920
Well, Jenna, I think this is a great time to take a break because when we come back, Dwight and Creed are going to catch a bat.

883
00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:09.680
All right, guys.

884
00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:11.840
So Creed and Dwight are on a mission.

885
00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:17.480
They are going to catch this bat before 6 p.m., before the professionals of animal control arrive.

886
00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:19.980
They're going to take care of this problem.

887
00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:23.960
And Kelly begs them because Creed has now gone into the supply closet.

888
00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:25.420
He's grabbing some kind of spray.

889
00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:27.700
And Kelly's like, do not hurt that bat.

890
00:42:27.880 --> 00:42:29.560
It has feelings.

891
00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:33.780
And she just makes this big plea for the bat's safety.

892
00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:37.480
And it comes flying into the main area.

893
00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:41.260
And then Kelly immediately screams, kill it, kill it, kill it.

894
00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:44.160
Yes, exactly.

895
00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:51.660
The bat flies into the little kitchen area and Kevin closes the door.

896
00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:58.280
So now that bat is trapped inside and Kevin is like, oh, my gosh, I am a hero.

897
00:42:58.860 --> 00:42:59.760
I am a hero.

898
00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:04.840
Kevin's day has gone from a near-death experience to being a hero.

899
00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.280
He is having a day.

900
00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:09.960
Why do I feel like that's like Kevin's every day?

901
00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:16.580
Every day Kevin thinks he's a hero over a really mundane thing that's not quite heroic.

902
00:43:17.380 --> 00:43:18.620
You might be right.

903
00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:19.520
Yeah.

904
00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:22.020
I mean, Kevin thinks he saved everyone in the office.

905
00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:25.960
But guys, not everyone was out of the kitchen.

906
00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:28.180
Meredith had gone to the bathroom.

907
00:43:28.660 --> 00:43:33.820
Well, we're going to leave her in there for a while because we're going back to Ryan's class.

908
00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:35.220
It's getting ugly.

909
00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:36.060
It's getting ugly.

910
00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:37.660
Michael is grasping at straws.

911
00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:38.640
He's so offended.

912
00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:39.860
He's taking it personal.

913
00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.440
And he's just started saying all kinds of stuff.

914
00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:43.120
Yeah.

915
00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:47.580
He's like, guys, this is a David and Goliath situation.

916
00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:50.120
But remember, David always wins.

917
00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.180
And it's just like America.

918
00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:55.180
America is facing five Goliaths.

919
00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:55.720
Five.

920
00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:57.400
And then he lists them off.

921
00:43:57.520 --> 00:44:02.140
It's like Al-Qaeda, global warming, sex predators, mercury poisoning.

922
00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:06.860
This is when Michael points out, you know what?

923
00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:09.640
Ryan has never made a sale.

924
00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:13.840
And he started a fire trying to make a cheesy PETA.

925
00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:15.360
That made me laugh out loud.

926
00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:17.720
And then he said, and everyone thinks he's a tease.

927
00:44:18.160 --> 00:44:18.620
Amazing.

928
00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:21.340
And then he says, you know what?

929
00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:24.120
Ryan doesn't know anything and neither do you.

930
00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:25.500
So suck on that.

931
00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:27.300
And he storms out of the classroom.

932
00:44:27.500 --> 00:44:28.400
He is so hurt.

933
00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:30.400
If you're Ryan, what are you thinking right now?

934
00:44:30.460 --> 00:44:34.400
Because you now know you rode there with him.

935
00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:38.080
You are going to have to ride back to work with this person.

936
00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:39.860
That is uncomfortable.

937
00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:43.280
Michael tells Ryan, business is always personal, Ryan.

938
00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:45.520
I mean, that says everything you need to know about Michael.

939
00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:46.540
But we know this.

940
00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:48.580
Well, I respect that about him.

941
00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.840
I respect that about Michael.

942
00:44:51.140 --> 00:44:52.840
That business isn't business.

943
00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:54.360
Business is always personal.

944
00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:57.000
And that, you know, he's going to say this later.

945
00:44:57.260 --> 00:44:58.920
A business is made up of people.

946
00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:00.060
I liked it.

947
00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:03.700
He tells Ryan, when we get back to the office, you better pack your things.

948
00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:07.060
This now starts one of my favorite storylines.

949
00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:10.020
Pam is finally at her art show.

950
00:45:10.420 --> 00:45:12.360
She's waiting for people to arrive.

951
00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:14.680
And we had a fan question.

952
00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:21.180
Alex Lane, Ryan Moberly, and Shelley Jones would like to know what kind of discussions

953
00:45:21.180 --> 00:45:23.880
occurred regarding Pam's art show outfit.

954
00:45:24.260 --> 00:45:27.280
And here, this little like swoopy side ponytail.

955
00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:32.300
Pam just is trying to look like she lives, I feel like, in the East Village of New York.

956
00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:38.240
And yes, well, they wanted to know if I had any input into what artsy Pam would look like

957
00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:38.800
that night.

958
00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:40.680
Oh, yes, I did.

959
00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:42.060
And it was so much fun.

960
00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:45.580
Carrie Bennett and I, we had the best time.

961
00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:52.160
She came up with this idea of kind of like this, this beatnik turtleneck.

962
00:45:52.400 --> 00:45:52.680
Yeah.

963
00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:56.020
With the little jumper over top.

964
00:45:56.020 --> 00:46:03.160
And then Kim Ferry and I thought, what is the hairstyle that coordinates with this little

965
00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:04.140
hipster outfit?

966
00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:06.200
And we thought low side pony.

967
00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:08.120
And it, I think it works.

968
00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:10.420
It's not just any low side pony, though.

969
00:46:10.520 --> 00:46:11.660
It's got a little bit of curl.

970
00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:14.020
It's kind of relaxed.

971
00:46:14.180 --> 00:46:14.400
Yeah.

972
00:46:14.580 --> 00:46:14.740
Yeah.

973
00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:15.860
It's smoothed out.

974
00:46:16.040 --> 00:46:19.900
It's not her like frizzy curly hair that we normally see her with.

975
00:46:20.020 --> 00:46:23.680
She is ready to walk out of that art show into 1960 something.

976
00:46:24.240 --> 00:46:25.280
It's so sweet.

977
00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:26.820
I absolutely loved it.

978
00:46:27.080 --> 00:46:30.160
And you can see all of Pam's art behind her.

979
00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:38.940
It's a series of watercolors of, you know, simple, ordinary items and things, which I think

980
00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:44.960
kind of goes to that whole theme of the show and Pam finding beauty in ordinary things.

981
00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:50.440
And I liked the juxtaposition that, you know, Pam's wall is like a coffee mug and a stapler

982
00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:55.500
and a vase holding flowers and, you know, a painting of Dunder Mifflin.

983
00:46:55.500 --> 00:47:02.620
And then just to the left, whoever's art display that was is like aliens or like an alien robot.

984
00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:04.240
I'm not really sure what it is.

985
00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:11.320
Well, we had a fan question from Molly Warner, who says, according to Dunderpedia, Joss Whedon

986
00:47:11.320 --> 00:47:17.160
was not satisfied with the original art that was produced for Pam at the art show and that

987
00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:18.740
new art had to be made.

988
00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:24.620
Can you clarify what that original art was and why it had to be changed?

989
00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:30.120
Well, we asked Brent about this and here's what he told us.

990
00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:37.100
What I remember most about Joss Whedon's directing of this episode was a crisis that happened around

991
00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:42.740
one of the watercolor drawings that appears in Pam's art show.

992
00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:48.160
Now, these watercolor drawings were created by the art department at the office and they appear

993
00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:51.040
in the background of all of the shots of Jenna.

994
00:47:51.380 --> 00:47:53.920
And, you know, as background items, they might not seem that important.

995
00:47:53.920 --> 00:48:01.640
But when we got on set, there was one drawing that Joss just strongly felt that Pam's character

996
00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.400
would not have done.

997
00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:10.220
It was a watercolor drawing of a church and he just felt it was out of character for her.

998
00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:14.280
And you have to imagine when you're shooting a television episode, the time pressure is

999
00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:15.120
so intense.

1000
00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:18.560
You do not have time to stop and mess around.

1001
00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:25.400
And Joss, however, felt so passionately about this picture that he stopped production and said,

1002
00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:26.620
I need a new picture.

1003
00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:29.540
I would like a picture of a stapler.

1004
00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:31.580
That's what I think that Pam would have drawn.

1005
00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:34.180
And like I say, the whole production shut down.

1006
00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:36.520
They brought the executive producer, Greg Daniels, to set.

1007
00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:40.040
They had a big discussion about it, but Joss would not back down.

1008
00:48:40.180 --> 00:48:41.580
And we were on location.

1009
00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:43.120
We didn't have the art department with us.

1010
00:48:43.200 --> 00:48:45.120
Who is going to draw a picture of a stapler?

1011
00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:49.220
So the assistant director, we were on a location at a university.

1012
00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:53.780
The assistant director went into a classroom full of art students and said,

1013
00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:56.240
is anyone here able to draw a watercolor of a stapler?

1014
00:48:56.500 --> 00:49:00.040
And they pulled some young woman out and they paid her 50 bucks or something.

1015
00:49:00.200 --> 00:49:04.480
And she just cranked out this picture of a stapler that you see in the background of all of Pam's shots.

1016
00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:09.240
And afterwards, I was riding back in the van, you know, to home base with Joss.

1017
00:49:09.400 --> 00:49:11.120
And I said, Joss, what was all that about?

1018
00:49:11.300 --> 00:49:13.640
Why did you get so intense about this?

1019
00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:17.200
And he said, Brent, in my experience as a director,

1020
00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:23.080
every single time that I have felt strongly about some detail of the shoot,

1021
00:49:23.080 --> 00:49:26.980
and I have allowed myself to be persuaded out of it because of time pressure,

1022
00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:32.960
100% of the time, I have intensely regretted making that choice.

1023
00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:37.600
And so now I understand that I should never compromise when I feel something strongly as a director.

1024
00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:39.340
It's a great lesson.

1025
00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:42.380
In my limited experience as a director, I've never forgotten it.

1026
00:49:42.380 --> 00:49:44.800
And I think it's a really good lesson for all of us as artists.

1027
00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:46.320
Stick to your guns.

1028
00:49:46.360 --> 00:49:48.980
If you feel it passionately, you're probably right.

1029
00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:51.020
I have to say, Angela,

1030
00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:58.540
the watercolor that stood out to me the most when we first flash up to her wall was that stapler.

1031
00:49:58.840 --> 00:49:59.820
Yeah, me too.

1032
00:49:59.820 --> 00:50:02.200
It was absolutely what Pam would have done.

1033
00:50:02.360 --> 00:50:06.820
Because Pam, we already established, she's sort of drawing things in her world.

1034
00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:09.880
And she sketched a tape dispenser.

1035
00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:11.480
It would make sense to me.

1036
00:50:11.660 --> 00:50:15.760
And then, you know, Jim, I feel like that stapler also represents Jim.

1037
00:50:16.080 --> 00:50:20.840
Jim put that stapler in Dwight's Jell-O mold, you know?

1038
00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:23.880
I never thought of that.

1039
00:50:23.980 --> 00:50:25.040
Yeah, that's what I thought.

1040
00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:29.020
But I thought Pam painted that stapler because she was thinking of Jim.

1041
00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:32.130
I just got chills.

1042
00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:36.890
Well, I got a little lowdown on the Dunder Mifflin painting from Kentopedia.

1043
00:50:37.230 --> 00:50:41.650
He said that that is not actually a watercolor,

1044
00:50:42.010 --> 00:50:48.590
but it was a photograph of our building that they manipulated to look like a watercolor.

1045
00:50:50.270 --> 00:50:53.370
Oh, like just some fancy kind of graphics.

1046
00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:54.950
Yeah.

1047
00:50:55.230 --> 00:51:02.490
So no one ever did a from blank page watercolor of the building.

1048
00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:08.730
You know, that time a while back, Jenna invited me to do a wine and canvas night.

1049
00:51:09.670 --> 00:51:10.350
Yes.

1050
00:51:10.710 --> 00:51:12.010
And it was so fun.

1051
00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:15.990
It was like part of a mall, but you entered from outside, right?

1052
00:51:16.490 --> 00:51:16.730
Yeah.

1053
00:51:16.810 --> 00:51:19.210
It was a mall in Pasadena.

1054
00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:20.030
Yeah.

1055
00:51:20.190 --> 00:51:24.470
And we went and you kind of just sort of pick a random night to go.

1056
00:51:24.650 --> 00:51:26.610
And I wasn't really sure what we were painting.

1057
00:51:26.890 --> 00:51:30.990
And then we found out we were painting cats on a fence at nighttime.

1058
00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:32.450
Yes.

1059
00:51:32.590 --> 00:51:33.930
And I still have mine.

1060
00:51:34.130 --> 00:51:38.210
My husband loves it so much, he hung it by where he feeds the cats.

1061
00:51:38.290 --> 00:51:42.190
So the cats see the cats on the fence as they eat their dinner.

1062
00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:43.030
It's kind of funny.

1063
00:51:43.030 --> 00:51:47.970
But I became sort of really interested in this wine and canvas night.

1064
00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:50.870
We were talking about doing another one, maybe inviting a few friends.

1065
00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:57.650
I found that they do wine and canvas and everyone can paint the Dunder Mifflin building.

1066
00:51:58.130 --> 00:52:00.310
Oh my gosh, that is amazing.

1067
00:52:00.630 --> 00:52:01.730
Wouldn't you want to do that?

1068
00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:03.230
I would love to do that.

1069
00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:09.150
Angela, I think it would be really weird if you and I showed up at a wine and canvas and

1070
00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:10.530
painted Dunder Mifflin.

1071
00:52:11.250 --> 00:52:12.650
There's like a room of people.

1072
00:52:12.830 --> 00:52:13.710
Like you sign up.

1073
00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:14.250
We showed up.

1074
00:52:14.350 --> 00:52:15.670
There were probably 30 people there.

1075
00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:17.950
And we had a little seating assignment.

1076
00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:19.490
We had our canvas and our paints.

1077
00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:21.510
And then they pass around wine.

1078
00:52:21.990 --> 00:52:23.610
And the teacher is at the front.

1079
00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:25.950
She had a headset on, remember, so we could all hear.

1080
00:52:26.290 --> 00:52:30.130
So you and I would be in a room with like 30 other people, total strangers.

1081
00:52:30.530 --> 00:52:35.590
I totally remember it because my daughter has always, from the time she was born, loved cats.

1082
00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:36.950
She loves cats.

1083
00:52:36.950 --> 00:52:38.990
And I knew she was going to love this painting.

1084
00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:43.650
And I didn't want my son to feel left out when I came home from my painting night.

1085
00:52:44.050 --> 00:52:49.270
So on my canvas, I added a little spaceship out.

1086
00:52:49.690 --> 00:52:50.130
Yes.

1087
00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:53.190
Out in the sky because he loved outer space.

1088
00:52:53.390 --> 00:52:56.510
So I was like, okay, well, this will bring him into this painting.

1089
00:52:56.690 --> 00:52:58.950
And we have it in our playroom.

1090
00:52:59.150 --> 00:52:59.970
I love it.

1091
00:52:59.990 --> 00:53:00.430
Hanging up.

1092
00:53:00.590 --> 00:53:01.170
But I do.

1093
00:53:01.350 --> 00:53:04.590
I want to do a wine and canvas night and I want to paint Dunder Mifflin, Jenna.

1094
00:53:05.070 --> 00:53:07.150
I can't say I want to do that.

1095
00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:10.330
You don't want to paint Dunder Mifflin?

1096
00:53:10.590 --> 00:53:11.610
I know.

1097
00:53:11.950 --> 00:53:13.550
Well, you have it, don't you?

1098
00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:14.470
You own it.

1099
00:53:14.570 --> 00:53:15.010
I do.

1100
00:53:15.370 --> 00:53:16.150
I already have.

1101
00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:17.110
You own it.

1102
00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:18.630
And I was going to leave that for the end.

1103
00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:19.610
You own it.

1104
00:53:19.730 --> 00:53:22.910
But also, Jenna, what if your painting was horrible?

1105
00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:28.770
I love your attempt at painting Dunder Mifflin.

1106
00:53:28.870 --> 00:53:29.450
No, I'm kidding.

1107
00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:32.230
Well, I will say this too.

1108
00:53:32.230 --> 00:53:35.070
My daughter's gotten really into watercolor painting.

1109
00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:44.830
And so it's very funny to me that I, as Jenna, am now sitting around every day doing watercolor paintings like my character Pam did.

1110
00:53:44.990 --> 00:53:47.910
And I think I've gotten really good at doing flowers.

1111
00:53:47.930 --> 00:53:50.170
I've actually watched some tutorial videos.

1112
00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:55.630
Well, maybe I'll watch a tutorial video on painting Dunder Mifflin and I'll...

1113
00:53:55.630 --> 00:53:56.870
There must be one.

1114
00:53:57.070 --> 00:53:58.670
I'll paint it and show it to you.

1115
00:53:59.490 --> 00:54:00.350
There you go.

1116
00:54:00.590 --> 00:54:00.850
Okay.

1117
00:54:00.910 --> 00:54:01.390
Sorry.

1118
00:54:01.570 --> 00:54:02.570
Where are we, lady?

1119
00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:10.350
Well, back at Dunder Mifflin, Meredith is still hiding from the bat and everyone is just leaving for the day.

1120
00:54:11.110 --> 00:54:12.210
Everyone's like, bye.

1121
00:54:12.730 --> 00:54:14.370
Creed is like, later, Mary Beth.

1122
00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:18.170
Yes, people wanted to know if that was improvisation.

1123
00:54:18.290 --> 00:54:19.030
No, that was scripted.

1124
00:54:19.370 --> 00:54:24.090
Well, there are a few deleted scenes that happen right here, Jenna, that are worth mentioning.

1125
00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:33.490
One is that there's a few cutaways that they didn't have time for of Meredith trying to leave the bathroom and then seeing the bat screaming and going back in.

1126
00:54:33.750 --> 00:54:34.810
Oh, okay.

1127
00:54:34.810 --> 00:54:39.490
You know, so kind of playing up the fact that she really does feel trapped and she really is scared.

1128
00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:39.770
Okay.

1129
00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:43.910
And then there's a scene of Dwight looking through the blinds and screaming at her.

1130
00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:47.030
So there's this sort of like buildup of this moment.

1131
00:54:47.290 --> 00:54:56.530
And then the other scene that I love that's deleted is as everyone's leaving for the day, leaving Meredith trapped, Angela and Kelly exit together.

1132
00:54:56.930 --> 00:55:00.570
And Angela says, just goes to show you the power of prayer.

1133
00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:03.690
And Kelly says, you prayed for this?

1134
00:55:03.690 --> 00:55:06.510
And Angela says, in a general sense.

1135
00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:08.310
Oh, my goodness.

1136
00:55:08.550 --> 00:55:13.130
So did I pray that Meredith needs some kind of reckoning and it's going to happen?

1137
00:55:13.470 --> 00:55:13.990
Wow.

1138
00:55:14.250 --> 00:55:14.670
I know.

1139
00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:20.330
I guess Angela maybe prayed that she would be scared into better behavior.

1140
00:55:20.830 --> 00:55:21.390
Yes.

1141
00:55:21.590 --> 00:55:24.690
And this is, she feels, the answer to her prayer.

1142
00:55:24.930 --> 00:55:26.510
Better lifestyle choices, Meredith.

1143
00:55:26.510 --> 00:55:29.750
So now we have the scene.

1144
00:55:30.030 --> 00:55:35.330
The scene of Dwight going in to capture this bat.

1145
00:55:36.790 --> 00:55:40.750
And it ends up landing on Meredith's head.

1146
00:55:40.750 --> 00:55:45.450
So he has to put a trash bag over her head with the bat inside.

1147
00:55:45.450 --> 00:55:48.250
And kind of like wrestle her around.

1148
00:55:48.770 --> 00:55:49.330
Insanity.

1149
00:55:49.570 --> 00:55:51.810
Well, you know, we got a lot of mail about this one, Angela.

1150
00:55:52.230 --> 00:55:58.850
Monica Sweeney, Jessica Bone, Ashley McGettrick, Aaron Westra, and Raphael Bresselsmith.

1151
00:55:58.950 --> 00:56:06.930
And I mean, just countless others said, the scene with Dwight trapping the bat on Meredith's head looked so real.

1152
00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:09.290
How did they do it?

1153
00:56:09.670 --> 00:56:12.570
Well, we asked Kate to tell us all about the scene.

1154
00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:16.970
And she told us she worked with both a real bat and a mechanical bat.

1155
00:56:17.410 --> 00:56:18.710
Here's what else she had to say.

1156
00:56:19.630 --> 00:56:21.090
Yes, we had a real bat.

1157
00:56:30.020 --> 00:56:33.200
We were not letting any noise around the bat.

1158
00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:34.460
I couldn't scream.

1159
00:56:34.780 --> 00:56:40.200
So even though I was screaming, I was acting like I was screaming when I was shooting because don't upset the bat.

1160
00:56:40.740 --> 00:56:41.840
Something about the sonar.

1161
00:56:41.840 --> 00:56:47.600
And then we had this motorized thing that I had on my head that we actually did have a stunt double.

1162
00:56:48.080 --> 00:56:51.020
But she, I don't really think they used her.

1163
00:56:51.380 --> 00:56:53.340
Maybe one little shot with her in it.

1164
00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:55.200
One cutaway.

1165
00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:57.480
But I did all of it.

1166
00:56:58.940 --> 00:57:04.380
The motorized thing on the head, it was sort of like this fluttery thing.

1167
00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:05.920
It was almost like a headband with a motor.

1168
00:57:06.500 --> 00:57:12.900
Um, and then there was a human being with a, like a puppety thing with a stick.

1169
00:57:13.140 --> 00:57:17.400
He was on his knees and he was trying to put the stick up my skirt before he introduced himself.

1170
00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:18.320
Which, whatever.

1171
00:57:18.540 --> 00:57:19.620
I mean, a little out of order.

1172
00:57:20.380 --> 00:57:23.720
Uh, and yes, I could breathe with a bag over my head.

1173
00:57:23.860 --> 00:57:24.520
We figured it out.

1174
00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:25.060
It was fine.

1175
00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:27.020
So I was acting like that.

1176
00:57:27.020 --> 00:57:35.460
And it was one of the most fun scenes I got to do with Rain because he is, he's an amazing actor and he goes for it, man.

1177
00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:38.500
So, but I'm not afraid.

1178
00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:41.900
I have never been afraid of Rainn Wilson or Dwight Schrute.

1179
00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:44.140
So that is, that's it.

1180
00:57:44.520 --> 00:57:46.280
Same bad time, same bad channel.

1181
00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:47.420
I just wanted to say that for him.

1182
00:57:49.400 --> 00:57:58.920
Yeah, I guess Phil Shea got this mechanical bat, like a battery-operated Halloween toy, and they put it on a headband on her head.

1183
00:57:59.260 --> 00:58:04.460
Because it really does look like there are wings flapping inside that bag.

1184
00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:09.500
It was, what a good, just old-fashioned movie magic moment.

1185
00:58:09.680 --> 00:58:14.380
Yeah, it was like some new technology and old tech all came together.

1186
00:58:15.040 --> 00:58:15.720
Yes.

1187
00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:21.940
I have a memory, Jenna, of like Kim Ferry and Phil Shea working on that headband.

1188
00:58:22.120 --> 00:58:25.700
Like, like he, I remember it in her hair and makeup area.

1189
00:58:25.880 --> 00:58:26.160
Do you?

1190
00:58:26.280 --> 00:58:28.940
Like, she was like sort of like futzing with it and stuff.

1191
00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:31.840
She had it on one of those little mannequins.

1192
00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:32.080
Yeah.

1193
00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:39.380
And he was in there with the little battery-operated controls explaining how it would work, and they would put a bag over it, and they would test it.

1194
00:58:39.560 --> 00:58:41.000
I remember that, too.

1195
00:58:41.080 --> 00:58:44.640
He must have been in there in the trailer when they were doing that.

1196
00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:45.820
I have that same memory.

1197
00:58:46.100 --> 00:58:46.280
Oh, man.

1198
00:58:46.480 --> 00:58:49.980
And then on top of it, there was a guy with a stick and a puppet.

1199
00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:50.680
My God.

1200
00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:52.660
What was that about?

1201
00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:53.420
No, I know.

1202
00:58:53.740 --> 00:58:56.100
I mean, Kate said, like, he had to put a stick up her skirt.

1203
00:58:56.220 --> 00:59:01.040
Like, maybe they had all these different ways the bat might be under the bag.

1204
00:59:02.020 --> 00:59:03.860
Anyway, well done, everyone.

1205
00:59:03.860 --> 00:59:05.100
It was a team effort.

1206
00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:07.060
But I thought that scene was fantastic.

1207
00:59:08.260 --> 00:59:13.220
Well, the other thing she said in her clip, too, Angela, was about how she wasn't allowed to actually scream.

1208
00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:13.860
Yes.

1209
00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:19.380
So, when I heard that, I remembered that from our big group scene with the bat.

1210
00:59:19.380 --> 00:59:20.180
Yes, I did, too.

1211
00:59:20.340 --> 00:59:23.580
When rain sort of, like, pokes the ceiling up, the real bat is up there.

1212
00:59:23.700 --> 00:59:25.240
That shot was the real bat.

1213
00:59:25.380 --> 00:59:27.120
And we all had to be silent.

1214
00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:32.580
And I remember we had to be super silent as they brought the bat in and placed the bat.

1215
00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:38.060
And then when they removed the bat, before we started doing all of our screaming and reacting to nothing.

1216
00:59:38.180 --> 00:59:39.640
That happens a lot with animals.

1217
00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:44.460
I worked with a squirrel where you had to be super quiet around the squirrel.

1218
00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:45.260
Really?

1219
00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:46.000
Yeah.

1220
00:59:46.340 --> 00:59:47.920
No sudden movements around the squirrel.

1221
00:59:48.820 --> 00:59:51.220
Okay, so now we're back at Pam's art show.

1222
00:59:51.420 --> 00:59:52.400
And Roy has come.

1223
00:59:52.540 --> 00:59:53.840
And he's brought his brother.

1224
00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:55.620
Yes.

1225
00:59:59.360 --> 01:00:05.120
And, you know, Roy has just regressed, right?

1226
01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:09.460
He says all of this stuff that is so insulting.

1227
01:00:09.580 --> 01:00:13.280
He's like, oh, hey, look at this.

1228
01:00:13.400 --> 01:00:15.840
Like, no one else showed up, but I came.

1229
01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:17.440
And I brought someone.

1230
01:00:18.160 --> 01:00:19.380
That's pretty cool.

1231
01:00:19.600 --> 01:00:24.160
Well, you know, I don't know if it's Roy regressing or is he just trying in his very

1232
01:00:25.340 --> 01:00:32.760
lame attempt here to say, Pam, look, you know, I show up for you, but.

1233
01:00:32.840 --> 01:00:38.400
I know, but like, what if he needs a big congratulations for showing up to his girlfriend's art show?

1234
01:00:38.620 --> 01:00:40.320
Like, you're doing the bare minimum.

1235
01:00:40.720 --> 01:00:47.940
Roy, at his real true self, is going to make the most basic amount of effort and expect that to be enough and even applauded.

1236
01:00:47.940 --> 01:00:58.060
Roy is the type of man who needs to be thanked profusely for doing everyday chores like the dishes and taking out the trash as if he's done something extraordinary.

1237
01:00:58.060 --> 01:01:01.740
But really, you're just contributing to the partnership of running a household.

1238
01:01:01.960 --> 01:01:02.100
Yeah.

1239
01:01:02.360 --> 01:01:03.580
I changed a light bulb.

1240
01:01:03.780 --> 01:01:03.900
Yeah.

1241
01:01:04.020 --> 01:01:04.640
Oh, my gosh.

1242
01:01:04.740 --> 01:01:04.980
Thank you.

1243
01:01:05.360 --> 01:01:12.200
Meanwhile, Roy is not probably thanking Pam for everything she's doing to contribute to running the household.

1244
01:01:12.380 --> 01:01:16.400
That's the dynamic that Pam has to look forward to if she follows through on this.

1245
01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:19.280
Well, I definitely think this art show night, she's raw.

1246
01:01:19.280 --> 01:01:27.840
She's feeling vulnerable and she needs Roy to be more of a support and he's just not able.

1247
01:01:28.040 --> 01:01:29.520
And I think that's that's the reality.

1248
01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:36.340
Roy is going to be a great partner to someone else that doesn't need the same things from him that Pam needs.

1249
01:01:37.240 --> 01:01:46.820
Well, as he's leaving, he tells her this famous line, which is that her art is the prettiest art of all the art.

1250
01:01:46.820 --> 01:01:49.340
Thank you, Roy, for that nugget.

1251
01:01:49.580 --> 01:01:52.680
So I do have a little post-it and I wrote on it.

1252
01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:54.040
I'm sorry.

1253
01:01:54.040 --> 01:01:55.740
Is this some Gil sass?

1254
01:01:56.140 --> 01:01:56.520
Yes.

1255
01:01:56.660 --> 01:01:57.540
Some Gil sass.

1256
01:01:57.820 --> 01:01:58.060
Yes.

1257
01:01:58.200 --> 01:02:01.160
Because Oscar and Gil show up to the art show.

1258
01:02:01.480 --> 01:02:04.020
They don't know that Pam has walked up behind them.

1259
01:02:04.580 --> 01:02:07.740
And Gil is really kind of going off on your art.

1260
01:02:07.880 --> 01:02:11.520
He's like, listen, real art takes courage and honesty.

1261
01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:13.360
And this is motel art.

1262
01:02:13.360 --> 01:02:17.300
And Oscar says those aren't Pam's strong points.

1263
01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:21.680
I think all of that hits her so harsh.

1264
01:02:21.920 --> 01:02:31.260
I think it's even more powerful for Pam to hear because in this moment, she's forced to hold up a mirror to her life.

1265
01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:39.800
And part of that courage and honesty is finally, boldly saying, Roy is not my person.

1266
01:02:39.800 --> 01:02:50.480
Well, Angela, earlier in this episode, Pam declares that being back with Roy shows maturity and dignity.

1267
01:02:50.740 --> 01:02:51.320
Remember?

1268
01:02:51.620 --> 01:02:53.760
And she's like, oh, I hope that didn't sound braggy.

1269
01:02:54.880 --> 01:03:08.220
But when she hears this from Gil and Oscar, she's being confronted with the fact that getting back with Roy does not show dignity or honesty or courage.

1270
01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:09.000
Yeah.

1271
01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:15.960
And I feel like it's one of these beautiful moments on our show where, you know, on the one hand, we're talking about her art.

1272
01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:23.720
But on the other hand, for Pam, it's like this self-realization about her choices in her life.

1273
01:03:23.720 --> 01:03:35.200
Well, you kind of think that it can't get lower for Pam because her only two visitors have been Roy and his brother and Oscar and Gil.

1274
01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:38.600
And then she's standing there alone.

1275
01:03:38.800 --> 01:03:40.280
The evening is winding down.

1276
01:03:40.760 --> 01:03:43.880
She starts to take her art off the wall.

1277
01:03:43.920 --> 01:03:46.000
But then Michael walks in.

1278
01:03:46.120 --> 01:03:47.060
And we hear,

1279
01:03:47.320 --> 01:03:48.780
Pam Casso!

1280
01:03:48.780 --> 01:03:49.860
And you're like, oh, no.

1281
01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:50.440
Oh, no.

1282
01:03:50.540 --> 01:03:51.740
Is this going to be good or bad?

1283
01:03:52.060 --> 01:03:54.180
He's started off with Pam Casso.

1284
01:03:55.360 --> 01:03:55.660
Yeah.

1285
01:03:55.920 --> 01:03:57.100
It's like, wow.

1286
01:03:57.440 --> 01:03:59.540
And I think that's Pam's reaction.

1287
01:03:59.820 --> 01:04:02.100
Pam's like, oh, Michael, you came.

1288
01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:06.720
And but it's kind of like, I don't even know if I can do this.

1289
01:04:06.920 --> 01:04:14.080
But Michael is immediately just so taken with her art.

1290
01:04:14.080 --> 01:04:17.680
And some of this is because of what he's been through today.

1291
01:04:18.340 --> 01:04:20.740
You know, Michael doesn't know what Pam's been through.

1292
01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:22.680
And Pam doesn't know what Michael's been through.

1293
01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:29.860
But in this moment, these two human beings are coming together and they heal one another.

1294
01:04:30.340 --> 01:04:30.500
Yeah.

1295
01:04:30.880 --> 01:04:31.300
Yeah.

1296
01:04:31.300 --> 01:04:34.840
They're coming together a little hurt and broken from the day.

1297
01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:38.700
And this is such a beautiful scene, Jenna.

1298
01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:40.460
I just love all the beats of it.

1299
01:04:40.560 --> 01:04:42.200
I love how excited Michael is.

1300
01:04:42.340 --> 01:04:45.180
I love how moved Pam is by his excitement.

1301
01:04:45.180 --> 01:04:49.420
And he sees that her car and his car, she painted both of them.

1302
01:04:49.560 --> 01:04:51.840
And it's just such a sweet scene.

1303
01:04:52.060 --> 01:04:53.800
We had a lot of people write in.

1304
01:04:54.080 --> 01:05:01.820
Mary Ruff, Soraya Garlip, Madison Schnurple, Stacey Dahl, and Layla Barocca all asked,

1305
01:05:02.040 --> 01:05:07.620
Jenna, how did you prepare for the level of emotion you had to play in this scene when

1306
01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:10.740
Michael shows up and tells Pam that he's proud of her?

1307
01:05:11.560 --> 01:05:14.140
Did you stay in that emotion during the breaks?

1308
01:05:14.400 --> 01:05:17.340
And if not, was it hard to get back between takes?

1309
01:05:18.820 --> 01:05:27.020
Guys, I will tell you, I didn't have to prepare much for this because my Steen partner was Steve

1310
01:05:27.020 --> 01:05:27.580
Carell.

1311
01:05:29.540 --> 01:05:36.260
And the relationship that I had with Steve in real life, and I think, Angela, this is a

1312
01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:43.200
little bit for you, too, he was someone I looked up to in the way that you look up to

1313
01:05:43.200 --> 01:05:44.700
a mentor or a parent.

1314
01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:49.020
And I admired him so much.

1315
01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:55.980
So just to have him look at me and say, I'm proud of you, I just started crying.

1316
01:05:56.420 --> 01:05:56.520
Yeah.

1317
01:05:56.520 --> 01:06:06.800
His sincerity and his truth in saying it, he's a wonderful actor, and it was easy.

1318
01:06:07.060 --> 01:06:07.580
Easy.

1319
01:06:07.900 --> 01:06:09.500
He's just such a special person.

1320
01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:15.460
So to have moments like that with him, the emotion is pretty ready to go.

1321
01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:20.880
I did this appearance on Busy Phillips Talk Show.

1322
01:06:21.020 --> 01:06:21.960
On Busy Tonight.

1323
01:06:21.960 --> 01:06:23.060
I remember.

1324
01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:31.240
Yeah, and it was like around my birthday, and she had secretly called people from the

1325
01:06:31.240 --> 01:06:36.140
office to send in gifts for me, and you sent in a shirt with a loaf of bread on it.

1326
01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:37.180
Here's the crazy thing.

1327
01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:38.400
She reached out to me.

1328
01:06:38.540 --> 01:06:39.940
She was like, Ange, I want to do this.

1329
01:06:40.280 --> 01:06:41.080
What do you think?

1330
01:06:41.080 --> 01:06:45.760
I said, I am literally online buying her a T-shirt with a loaf of bread on it.

1331
01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:48.680
And she was like, oh my God, can we have that?

1332
01:06:50.300 --> 01:06:55.280
So it was this total surprise, and I get all these presents, and this whole time that I've

1333
01:06:55.280 --> 01:07:00.800
been doing this talk show appearance, the entire time, there has been a giant box sitting

1334
01:07:00.800 --> 01:07:01.720
on the set.

1335
01:07:01.840 --> 01:07:03.340
For like 20 minutes.

1336
01:07:03.740 --> 01:07:03.940
Right?

1337
01:07:04.140 --> 01:07:04.800
20 minutes.

1338
01:07:04.920 --> 01:07:05.320
Easily.

1339
01:07:05.660 --> 01:07:09.680
And at the end of the segment, she says, well, we have one last gift for you.

1340
01:07:10.640 --> 01:07:18.180
And it's this giant box, and the box pops open, and Steve Carell pops out of the box.

1341
01:07:18.340 --> 01:07:23.700
First of all, I was so scared because I wasn't expecting a human being to come out of the box.

1342
01:07:23.900 --> 01:07:29.840
And then as soon as I saw that it was Steve, I just like immediately burst into tears.

1343
01:07:30.060 --> 01:07:35.900
He had sat in a box for like 20 minutes while I droned on giving this interview.

1344
01:07:36.760 --> 01:07:38.980
Just to surprise me for my birthday.

1345
01:07:39.300 --> 01:07:40.160
I don't know.

1346
01:07:40.420 --> 01:07:42.620
It's like, that's Steve, you guys.

1347
01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:47.720
It was such a just generous, like, kind, loving thing to do.

1348
01:07:47.940 --> 01:07:51.400
So you just have to, that's how he's always been.

1349
01:07:51.600 --> 01:07:56.360
So in this moment, shooting the scene with him or any emotional scene with him, it's all

1350
01:07:56.360 --> 01:07:57.080
just right there.

1351
01:07:57.220 --> 01:07:58.960
I love that so much, Jenna.

1352
01:07:59.100 --> 01:08:02.300
I hope people can still watch that because that moment is so sweet.

1353
01:08:02.300 --> 01:08:10.220
And you start to cry, and Steve is smiling, and you guys hug, and you say, how long have

1354
01:08:10.220 --> 01:08:11.120
you been in there?

1355
01:08:11.240 --> 01:08:12.900
And he's like, for a while.

1356
01:08:13.100 --> 01:08:16.500
Because they had to set that box before you came out.

1357
01:08:16.720 --> 01:08:17.240
Yes.

1358
01:08:17.400 --> 01:08:20.880
So he was there before the interview and through the whole interview.

1359
01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:21.480
Yes.

1360
01:08:21.700 --> 01:08:27.080
Well, we asked Brent about the art show and how they created this moment between Michael

1361
01:08:27.080 --> 01:08:36.180
and Pam and Jenna, you might want to buckle in because get ready for a love fest from Brent

1362
01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:36.680
Forrester.

1363
01:08:36.960 --> 01:08:41.840
When people ask me what is my favorite episode of TV that I have ever written, I always say

1364
01:08:41.840 --> 01:08:42.600
business school.

1365
01:08:43.240 --> 01:08:46.180
And partly it's because the episode is so nicely designed.

1366
01:08:46.180 --> 01:08:50.680
It's got that big comedy sea story with the bat and the vampire.

1367
01:08:50.680 --> 01:08:55.840
It's got a big comedy performance story with Michael going to business school.

1368
01:08:56.040 --> 01:09:00.500
But above all, it has this tremendous emotional story with Pam's art show.

1369
01:09:00.920 --> 01:09:05.940
I think that what is happening in Pam's art show is one of those wonderful coincidences

1370
01:09:05.940 --> 01:09:11.360
of the experience of the writer, me, and the actress, Jenna, coming together.

1371
01:09:11.640 --> 01:09:17.800
If you try to be an artist of any kind, a writer, an actress, any artist at all, you have

1372
01:09:17.800 --> 01:09:24.580
to believe in yourself so profoundly and inevitably there will come moments of failure where your

1373
01:09:24.580 --> 01:09:29.960
belief in yourself is dashed and it is so painful and unforgettable when it happens to

1374
01:09:29.960 --> 01:09:30.500
a human being.

1375
01:09:30.940 --> 01:09:33.700
And I think it's really what Jenna is expressing at the end of this episode.

1376
01:09:33.860 --> 01:09:38.620
If you read her tremendous book, An Actor's Life, which is both an instructional manual and

1377
01:09:38.620 --> 01:09:45.380
a riveting autobiography, you will learn that Jenna Fisher spent seven years trying to break

1378
01:09:45.380 --> 01:09:46.960
into the business of acting.

1379
01:09:47.200 --> 01:09:50.000
At the end of seven years, she felt that she had failed,

1380
01:09:50.200 --> 01:09:53.940
she packed up her car, and she decided she was going to return home to Missouri.

1381
01:09:54.640 --> 01:09:58.320
She was only persuaded to stay by her acting coach and her manager who said,

1382
01:09:58.540 --> 01:10:02.600
please, Jenna, spend one more season trying out for TV, and that's when she got the office.

1383
01:10:02.840 --> 01:10:07.040
But that experience of terrible defeat is something that I believe she never forgot,

1384
01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:09.500
and it's what she's expressing at the end of this episode.

1385
01:10:09.500 --> 01:10:16.000
Steve Carell says that when he watches Business School, that moment where Michael Scott hugs Pam

1386
01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:22.160
and there's a close on her face, Steve says he can see five different emotions play through

1387
01:10:22.160 --> 01:10:23.940
Jenna's face in that performance.

1388
01:10:23.960 --> 01:10:26.940
It is heartbreak and it is gratitude.

1389
01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:29.540
It is just the whole human

1390
01:10:29.540 --> 01:10:32.200
experience wrapped up in a single take.

1391
01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:35.860
It's a tremendous performance, and it's part of why I will

1392
01:10:35.860 --> 01:10:41.820
always be so grateful that I got to write this episode for one of my favorite actresses of all

1393
01:10:41.820 --> 01:10:42.860
time, Jenna Fisher.

1394
01:10:43.300 --> 01:10:44.600
Oh my gosh, Brent.

1395
01:10:44.900 --> 01:10:47.260
What are you going to say after that?

1396
01:10:47.440 --> 01:10:48.600
That is the most beautiful

1397
01:10:48.600 --> 01:10:50.060
thing ever.

1398
01:10:50.780 --> 01:10:53.560
Jenna, that is so lovely.

1399
01:10:54.600 --> 01:10:55.540
You know what?

1400
01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.420
I am going to save that, and I'm going to listen to

1401
01:10:58.420 --> 01:11:07.020
that any time I am having a bad day because that really, thank you, that lifts me up.

1402
01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:09.660
Jenna, I have been at all of your big life moments.

1403
01:11:09.860 --> 01:11:10.600
Really, I have.

1404
01:11:10.740 --> 01:11:12.940
I have heard toast and speeches

1405
01:11:12.940 --> 01:11:18.800
about you, and that's probably the beSt. I was at your wedding.

1406
01:11:19.200 --> 01:11:20.660
Brent should have given a toast at

1407
01:11:20.660 --> 01:11:21.100
your wedding.

1408
01:11:21.560 --> 01:11:22.160
Clearly.

1409
01:11:22.560 --> 01:11:22.980
Clearly.

1410
01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:24.260
Oh my gosh.

1411
01:11:24.340 --> 01:11:27.540
He like recapped your book and your whole life experience,

1412
01:11:27.540 --> 01:11:30.920
and then what Steve said was so beautiful.

1413
01:11:31.600 --> 01:11:33.860
I mean, Jenna, Brent was going on for so long.

1414
01:11:34.080 --> 01:11:34.560
He ran out

1415
01:11:34.560 --> 01:11:34.940
of air.

1416
01:11:35.120 --> 01:11:35.520
Did you hear?

1417
01:11:35.660 --> 01:11:36.300
At the end, he had to go.

1418
01:11:37.460 --> 01:11:40.080
He was so passionate talking about you.

1419
01:11:40.420 --> 01:11:41.500
Everything he said is true.

1420
01:11:41.620 --> 01:11:42.100
There's one thing.

1421
01:11:42.220 --> 01:11:44.700
I never actually packed up my car and said I was

1422
01:11:44.700 --> 01:11:50.200
going to leave, but I did say I called my managers after seven years of failures and said I've decided

1423
01:11:50.200 --> 01:11:51.620
to become a vet technician.

1424
01:11:52.300 --> 01:11:53.680
I had signed up.

1425
01:11:53.780 --> 01:11:55.240
It was a two-year program.

1426
01:11:55.380 --> 01:11:56.820
I'd been doing some animal

1427
01:11:57.700 --> 01:12:02.420
and I was going to do it full-time, and I wanted my vet tech license so that I could administer

1428
01:12:02.420 --> 01:12:02.900
medicines.

1429
01:12:02.940 --> 01:12:05.360
And so I was like, I'm out of here.

1430
01:12:05.940 --> 01:12:10.340
And it is true that my manager and my acting coach

1431
01:12:11.160 --> 01:12:15.040
sort of yelled at me, and they were like, what did you think an acting career was?

1432
01:12:15.040 --> 01:12:15.740
You thought it was

1433
01:12:15.740 --> 01:12:16.700
just a bunch of ups?

1434
01:12:16.920 --> 01:12:19.600
Just a bunch of successes?

1435
01:12:20.020 --> 01:12:20.300
No.

1436
01:12:20.820 --> 01:12:23.640
It's little successes followed by many failures.

1437
01:12:23.640 --> 01:12:25.840
This is what being an actor is.

1438
01:12:25.960 --> 01:12:30.820
And it's true what Brent says too, which is that I did channel a lot

1439
01:12:30.820 --> 01:12:33.960
of those struggling years into my performance for Pam.

1440
01:12:34.160 --> 01:12:37.400
I literally sat at a reception desk

1441
01:12:37.400 --> 01:12:41.840
and dreamed of being an artiSt. I have done that.

1442
01:12:42.000 --> 01:12:43.360
I did it for seven years.

1443
01:12:43.620 --> 01:12:46.620
So I deeply, deeply felt

1444
01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:47.700
Pam's longing.

1445
01:12:48.960 --> 01:12:54.960
Brent also sent us in an audio clip where he discussed just the overall story for this

1446
01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:59.820
episode with Pam and Michael and why he thinks that it resonates so much with people.

1447
01:13:00.180 --> 01:13:01.680
And here's what

1448
01:13:01.680 --> 01:13:03.020
he had to say about that.

1449
01:13:03.380 --> 01:13:07.720
I remember before I was sent off to write this episode, I got into a big

1450
01:13:07.720 --> 01:13:12.340
debate with Greg Daniels, the show creator, about the story of Pam's art show.

1451
01:13:12.520 --> 01:13:13.320
And I, at the time,

1452
01:13:13.320 --> 01:13:15.580
thought of myself as a sophisticated storyteller.

1453
01:13:15.680 --> 01:13:18.200
And I took the position that this story did not

1454
01:13:18.200 --> 01:13:23.880
work because, I said to Greg, a story is about a protagonist pursuing a goal, overcoming obstacles,

1455
01:13:24.060 --> 01:13:25.620
and in the end, accomplishing that goal.

1456
01:13:25.780 --> 01:13:29.260
And in this case, Pam's goal is to be respected as an artiSt.

1457
01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:34.480
But when Michael Scott buys her painting at the end, it doesn't resolve her problem because she does

1458
01:13:34.480 --> 01:13:36.720
not respect Michael Scott's taste.

1459
01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:40.780
Ipso facto, her problem is not resolved and the story does not work.

1460
01:13:40.780 --> 01:13:46.380
And I remember Greg saying to me, Brent, this is a sophisticated story with a sophisticated ending.

1461
01:13:46.600 --> 01:13:47.720
It would be like this.

1462
01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:52.280
Suppose you, Brent, were the protagonist and your goal was to impress me

1463
01:13:52.280 --> 01:13:53.500
with your storytelling abilities.

1464
01:13:53.580 --> 01:13:56.300
But you said something so stupid in the breaking of this story

1465
01:13:56.300 --> 01:13:58.060
that I fired you and sent you home.

1466
01:13:58.240 --> 01:14:00.980
You went home miserable, not having accomplished your goal,

1467
01:14:01.120 --> 01:14:06.100
but your family dog came up to you, cuddled with you on the couch, and put its head on your lap.

1468
01:14:06.100 --> 01:14:12.440
This would be an emotional resolution and consolation, even though your stated goal was

1469
01:14:12.440 --> 01:14:13.220
not achieved.

1470
01:14:13.580 --> 01:14:18.080
Greg was so right about this, and it was so funny the way he put it.

1471
